### Description

The cache subscriber map entries are never deleted.

https://github.com/posit-dev/launcher-go-sdk/blob/9c614a5da865acba12e3dac4a4b754440abab0a6/cache/cache.go#L24

The `subscribeToID` map is the most concerning, as it grows based on the number of jobs. Subscription is append-only from the subscriber's side. The subscriber never says, "I'm done listening" and the subscriber channel is never closed.

Observed while reviewing https://github.com/posit-dev/launcher-go-sdk/pull/39.

### Steps to Reproduce

This (Claude-written) test demonstrates that the cache grows, but it's not perfect since the cache doesn't appear to expose the right lifecycle controls.

```go
package cache

import (
	"context"
	"log/slog"
	"testing"

	"github.com/posit-dev/launcher-go-sdk/api"
)

// TestSubscriberMapEntryReclaimed asserts the behavior the cache should have:
// once a job is finished and its subscribers have gone, the per-ID subManager
// in updatesByID should be reclaimed. Over the life of a plugin that streams
// status for many distinct job IDs, retaining this state grows the map without
// bound.
//
// The test drives both natural end-of-life signals — the job reaches a terminal
// status, and its consumer cancels — without relying on any further activity,
// because in reality none occurs after a job finishes.
//
// This is a TDD/regression test and FAILS against the current implementation,
// which has no reclamation path for either signal:
//   - There is no terminal/"job done" hook, so a finished job's subManager is
//     never freed.
//   - A canceled subscriber is only pruned from the list lazily, on the *next*
//     Notify for that job — but a terminal job produces no further updates, so
//     the defunct subscriber lingers too.
//
// Both the empty/defunct subManager and its map key persist until Close(). It
// should pass once a finished job (or its last departing subscriber) reclaims
// the entry.
func TestSubscriberMapEntryReclaimed(t *testing.T) {
	lgr := slog.New(slog.DiscardHandler)
	c, err := NewJobCache(lgr)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("NewJobCache: %v", err)
	}
	defer func() {
		if err := c.Close(); err != nil {
			t.Errorf("Close: %v", err)
		}
	}()

	const id api.JobID = "j1"
	ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
	ch := make(chan *statusUpdate, 1)
	c.subscribeToID(ctx, id, ch)

	// The job reaches a terminal status. This is the last update the cache will
	// ever produce for it; the subscriber receives it.
	c.processUpdate(&statusUpdate{ID: id, Status: api.StatusFinished})

	// Having observed the terminal status, the consumer stops listening.
	cancel()

	// The job is done and has no live consumer, yet nothing has reclaimed its
	// subscriber bookkeeping.
	c.mu.RLock()
	mgr, ok := c.updatesByID[id]
	tracked := 0
	if ok {
		tracked = mgr.Count()
	}
	c.mu.RUnlock()

	if ok {
		t.Errorf("subscriber state for %q was not reclaimed after the job "+
			"finished and its consumer canceled (still tracking %d subscriber(s)); "+
			"updatesByID grows without bound", id, tracked)
	}
}
```

