### Description

When cache subscribers receive updates faster than they are processed, the newest update is dropped. This might cause subscribers to miss state transitions.

Observed while reviewing #18; Claude helped create the test, which might need adjusting if the cache implementation changes.

### Steps to Reproduce

```go
package cache

import (
	"context"
	"log/slog"
	"testing"
	"time"

	"github.com/posit-dev/launcher-go-sdk/api"
)

// TestNoStateTransitionMissed asserts the behavior the cache should have: a
// subscriber that keeps reading observes every status transition the cache
// produces. Each message the cache publishes is a genuine state change — the
// producer only calls notify() when Status or StatusMsg actually changes — so
// no message is redundant or safe to discard.
//
// This is a TDD/regression test and FAILS against the current implementation.
// subManager.Notify delivers with a non-blocking send onto a cap-1 channel:
// when the buffer is full the *newest* update is dropped while the stale
// buffered value is retained. A subscriber that briefly falls behind therefore
// misses real transitions — and is left holding obsolete state rather than the
// latest. The terminal transition is merely the most damaging instance (nothing
// follows it), but any missed transition is a correctness loss.
func TestNoStateTransitionMissed(t *testing.T) {
	lgr := slog.New(slog.DiscardHandler)
	c, err := NewJobCache(lgr)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("NewJobCache: %v", err)
	}
	defer func() {
		if err := c.Close(); err != nil {
			t.Errorf("Close: %v", err)
		}
	}()

	const id api.JobID = "j1"
	// Same buffer size RunningJobContext/StreamJobStatus use.
	ch := make(chan *statusUpdate, 1)
	c.subscribeToID(context.Background(), id, ch)

	// A real job progresses through several distinct states. The subscriber is
	// momentarily slow (not draining), modeling any consumer that falls behind.
	produced := []string{
		api.StatusPending,
		api.StatusRunning,
		api.StatusSuspended,
		api.StatusFinished,
	}
	for _, status := range produced {
		// processUpdate is the same delivery path the service goroutine runs
		// for every cached update.
		c.processUpdate(&statusUpdate{ID: id, Status: status})
	}

	// The subscriber now drains everything it was actually given.
	//
	// Nothing closes ch on this path (the subscriber's context is live and
	// Close runs only in the deferred cleanup), so we cannot read until the
	// channel is closed. An immediate select/default would instead assume
	// delivery is synchronous: it would treat an empty buffer as end-of-stream
	// and could race a send handed off to another goroutine. As a compromise we
	// wait a bounded quiescence window for each next update; if none arrives we
	// treat the stream as drained. This tolerates an async handoff shorter than
	// the window at the cost of paying it once per run.
	const quiesce = 250 * time.Millisecond
	var observed []string
	for {
		select {
		case u := <-ch:
			observed = append(observed, u.Status)
		case <-time.After(quiesce):
			goto done
		}
	}
done:

	// Every produced transition should have been observable.
	if len(observed) != len(produced) {
		t.Errorf("subscriber observed %d of %d state transitions; missed %v\n"+
			"  produced: %v\n  observed: %v",
			len(observed), len(produced),
			missing(produced, observed), produced, observed)
	}
}

// missing returns the elements of produced not present in observed, preserving
// order. Order-insensitive membership is sufficient here: the point is which
// transitions never reached the subscriber.
func missing(produced, observed []string) []string {
	seen := make(map[string]int, len(observed))
	for _, s := range observed {
		seen[s]++
	}
	var miss []string
	for _, s := range produced {
		if seen[s] > 0 {
			seen[s]--
			continue
		}
		miss = append(miss, s)
	}
	return miss
}
```

